.TH PLASMA\-BUILTIN\-SCREEN\-ROTATION 1 "2026" "asus\-zenbook\-duo\-ux8406" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation \- turn the built\-in screens with the laptop in KDE Plasma
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation
.RB [ run | restore ]
.br
.B plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation
.BR \-h " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation
turns the built\-in screens of a laptop when the laptop stands on its left or
right side or upside down, also with a keyboard, a touchpad or a mouse
connected. On its side, two built\-in screens go side by side, in the order in
which they lie. When the laptop is upright again, the layout from before comes
back. Upside down, the screens are turned by 180 degrees and go one below the
other, the bottom one on top.
.PP
Each built\-in screen can also be turned by hand, on its own, to any of the four
orientations (method
.BR TurnScreen ).
That puts Auto\-rotate off for the session: the sensor is not followed, so that
it cannot undo the turn, and the property
.B Enabled
is false. The saved setting does not change, so that after a new login the
screens follow the sensor again. The methods
.B SetEnabled
(with true) and
.B Toggle
switch Auto\-rotate on again: all screens go back under the sensor and turn to
the orientation of the laptop at once. The screens stay as they lie, one below
the other or side by side, in the same order. When the monitors change (the
keyboard goes on or off the lower screen), each held screen that is shown is
turned to its orientation again; if KWin does not take that, the screen is not
held any more.
.PP
The program is made for the two screens of the ASUS Zenbook Duo UX8406, but it
operates on a laptop of any model. It needs KDE Plasma 6.4 or later on Wayland,
and
.BR iio\-sensor\-proxy (8)
for the orientation.
.PP
An XDG autostart entry starts the program in each Plasma session. The applet
\(lqAuto\-rotate\(rq in the system tray turns the rotation on and off with a middle click,
and a left click opens its popup. The program also registers the global shortcut
\(lqToggle auto\-rotate\(rq with kglobalacceld, in the component
\(lqAuto\-rotate screens\(rq, with no default key; set a key in System Settings,
Shortcuts. The journal says who switched the rotation, for example
\(lqrotation off (shortcut)\(rq. When no sensor
gives the orientation, the applet shows two buttons that turn the
screens by 90 degrees.
.PP
KWin turns only the first built\-in screen by itself. While the program runs, it
sets KWin's setting \(lqAutomatic orientation\(rq to \(lqNever\(rq on each
built\-in screen, and keeps the value from before in its configuration file.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Follow the orientation of the laptop until the session ends. This is the
default.
.TP
.B restore
Stop the program of this session and give each built\-in screen KWin's
\(lqAutomatic orientation\(rq setting from before. Run it as the user, in the
session, before the package is removed. A screen that is not connected keeps
its saved value in the file until the next
.BR restore .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation.toml
Whether the rotation is on, and KWin's setting from before for each built\-in
screen. Without
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
the file is in
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/plasma\-builtin\-screen\-rotation.desktop
The entry that starts the program in each session.
.TP
.I ~/.config/kwinoutputconfig.json
KWin's saved layouts. A turned layout stays there until the next login; the
program then puts the screens as the laptop stands.
.SH D\-BUS
The program owns the name
.B io.github.cceelen.BuiltinScreenRotation
on the session bus. It has the properties
.BR Enabled ,
.B Control
.RB ( switch ", " buttons " or " none ),
.B CanTurnClockwise
and
.BR CanTurnCounterclockwise ,
and the methods
.BR SetEnabled ,
.BR Toggle ,
.BR TurnScreen ,
.B Turn
and
.BR Quit .
.B Enabled
is false while a screen is held, whatever the saved setting says.
.B Screens
is an array of structures (connector, name, orientation) of the built\-in
screens that are on, in the order of the compositor's list:
.I Upper screen
and
.I Lower screen
for two screens,
.I Built\-in screen
for one. The orientation is
.BR normal ,
.BR left\-up ,
.BR right\-up
or
.BR bottom\-up ,
and empty for a mirrored screen. The property signals its changes.
.BI TurnScreen( connector , orientation )
turns one screen, and the call fails with an error for an unknown orientation.
.B Turn
turns all screens by 90 degrees and ends the hold.
.B Toggle
switches the rotation on when it is off, and off when it is on: the program
holds the state. Each change of the state is one line in the journal, with the
method that asked. After
.BR Quit ,
the program changes no setting and no layout of KWin, and ends.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the session ends, or when
.B restore
is done. 1 when the program cannot use the session bus or KWin's outputs, when
an instance runs already, when the configuration file cannot be read or
written, or when KWin does not take the settings back. The journal gives the
cause.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kscreen\-doctor (1),
.BR iio\-sensor\-proxy (8),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
